tool_calls.instructions:
Test the GraphQL endpoint at POST http://juiceshop.local:3000/graphql for vulnerabilities.

Recon found that POST /graphql returned the Angular shell (not functional GraphQL). But let me verify this more carefully and also check if there's a different GraphQL path.

### 1. GraphQL Endpoint Verification
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{__typename}"}
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{securityQuestion(userEmail: \"test@test.com\")}"}
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{users {id email password}}"}
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{products {id name price}}"}
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{orders {id}}"}
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{challenge(key: \"test\")}"}

### 2. Check if there's a different GraphQL path
- POST /api/graphql
- POST /juice-shop/graphql
- POST /graphql?query={__typename}

### 3. GraphQL Introspection
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{__schema {types {name fields {name type {name}}}}}"}

### 4. SQL Injection via GraphQL
If GraphQL is functional, try:
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"{products(filter: {name: {like: \"%' OR 1=1--\"}})}"}

### 5. XSS via GraphQL
If GraphQL accepts and stores input, try:
- POST /graphql with body: {"query":"mutation {createFeedback(message: \"<script>alert(1)</script>\") {id}}"}

Save all findings with evidence. Document which GraphQL endpoints are functional and which are not.

